 ## Features
 
-**The frequency band and rooms.** Telsiz is laid out like a radio dial. The frequencies you have joined sit as stations on a horizontal frequency band at the top of the screen. A needle above the band shows the open frequency, and pressing another station or dragging and dropping the needle with a mouse or a finger switches to that frequency. Each station shows the status of the frequency (open, online, offline, sign-in needed) and its unread and mention counts. The text and voice rooms of the open frequency are in the Stations list in the right column: unread and mention badges, the number of people in a voice room and, under the room, the people in it (avatar, name, mute and camera icons, a ring around the person speaking), a speaking indicator and the Telsiz DJ note, and an Add room button for people with the manage rooms permission. While Telsiz DJ is playing, the list stays at the top of the right column and the DJ card sits below it. On narrow screens and phones the list opens from the Stations button in the top bar. On wide screens the radio card sits at the bottom of the left column with the Notifications list above it, and the wide conversation column sits in the middle. The Notifications list shows screen shares that start in your voice room with a Watch button, and people joining and leaving voice rooms in short lines. The top bar has the frequency name on the left with the Online button right next to it, Search in the very middle, and the Direct messages and Friends buttons and the avatar menu on the right. Your profile, status and settings are in the avatar menu. The ? button at the end of the band opens the guide to changing frequencies.
+**The frequency band and rooms.** Telsiz is laid out like a radio dial. The frequencies you have joined sit as stations on a horizontal frequency band at the top of the screen. A needle above the band shows the open frequency, and pressing another station or dragging and dropping the needle with a mouse or a finger switches to that frequency. Each station shows the status of the frequency (open, online, offline, sign-in needed) and its unread and mention counts. The text and voice rooms of the open frequency are in the Stations list in the right column: a frequency readout to the left of every room like a channel preset, unread and mention badges, the number of people in a voice room and, under the room, the people in it as chips side by side (avatar, name, mute and camera icons, a ring around the person speaking), a speaking indicator and the Telsiz DJ note, and an Add room button for people with the manage rooms permission. While Telsiz DJ is playing, the list stays at the top of the right column and the DJ card sits below it. On narrow screens and phones the list opens from the Stations button in the top bar. On wide screens the radio card fills the left column like a handheld radio with the Notifications list right above it, and the wide conversation column sits in the middle. Outside a voice room the radio card shows the voice rooms with Join buttons. The Notifications list shows screen shares that start in your voice room with a Watch button, and people joining and leaving voice rooms in short lines. The top bar has the frequency name on the left with the Online button right next to it, Search in the very middle, and the Direct messages and Friends buttons and the avatar menu on the right. Your profile, status and settings are in the avatar menu. The ? button at the end of the band opens the guide to changing frequencies.
 
 **Several frequencies.** The + button of the band adds a new frequency address (`https://`, or `http://localhost` for a server on this computer), and the All button lists the frequencies with their status and counts in a sheet, where they can be removed from the list. The frequency name on the left of the top bar shows the details of the open frequency and shortcuts to its settings. In the desktop app each frequency keeps its own sign-in, switching happens in the same window, and the app stays connected in the background to the 8 most recently used frequencies to show their unread counts and mentions. In the browser each frequency is a separate site: the list is kept in that browser, switching takes the tab to the other address, and the list travels to the other frequency in the `#` part of the address (only addresses and names, never keys). Frequencies added to the list this way are announced with their addresses and do not change the order of an existing list, and a new frequency that carries the name of another frequency in the list shows its address instead of that name. In the browser the status, notifications and unread counts of frequencies that are not open are not shown. Switching ends the voice connection (you are asked first while in a voice room), and the list is not synced between devices.
 
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@ Messages, files, profiles and the setup messages of voice connections are encryp
 
 **Profile.** Settings > Profile sets the display name, custom status, about me text, profile color, profile picture and cover photo. The profile picture is center-cropped to a square and re-encoded at 256x256, the cover photo is center-cropped to 3:1 and re-encoded at 1200x400, and metadata such as the location is removed. The profile details and both images are end-to-end encrypted and only members with the encryption key can see them. The cover appears in the color band of the profile card and is downloaded only when the card is opened, so the server can see who opens whose card.
 
-**Voice chat.** Audio flows directly between people over WebRTC and does not pass through the server. You choose between voice activity detection (with an automatic or manual threshold) and push to talk. The choice can also be made from a small menu that opens when you right click the microphone button (Shift+F10 on the keyboard). A keyboard key, a mouse side button or a gamepad button can be assigned to push to talk, mute and deafen. When you join a voice room, a radio card that looks like a handheld radio opens: the people in the room, a halo around the speaker, a large Push to talk button, and buttons in two rows, each in its own color (Camera, Microphone and Deafen on top, Screen and Leave below, on phones only icons in a single row). The volume of each person can be set between 0% and 200%, and anything above 100% boosts that person's voice on the device. Because the boosted voice plays from the browser's audio context, Chromium's echo cancellation may not take it into account, so echo may reach the other side when you use speakers. Headphones avoid this. A distinct 2 second notification sound plays when someone joins or leaves the voice room, when someone starts a screen share, when a direct message or a friend request arrives and when you drop out of the voice room. The sounds are not played from files, they are synthesized on the device. Their volume is set with the slider in Settings > Notifications (40% by default), where each sound can also be played. Advanced noise suppression (RNNoise) suppresses non-speech sounds such as keyboard clicks on the device before they reach others, and it is on by default. By default up to 8 people can join a voice room, and the owner of the frequency can change this limit between 2 and 12 people in Settings > General.
